course: medicine
lesson: 1
title: Snap! Breaking a bone
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Bones are hard and strong, but a big force at a bad angle, like landing on your hand when you fall off the climbing frame, can crack or snap one.
explain: Children's bones are bendier than grown-ups', so they often break like a green twig: bent on one side and cracked on the other.
explain: You cannot always tell a break by wiggling it, and you do not always hear a crack; only an X-ray shows the bone for sure.
explain: Bones heal in whatever position they are held in, so doctors line the pieces up and hold them still for weeks while new bone grows.
explain: If you think a bone is broken: keep it still, tell a grown-up at once, and stop playing.
fact: A child's bone often breaks like a green twig, bending on one side and cracking on the other; this is called a greenstick fracture.
fact: Keeping a broken arm still stops the broken ends moving, which hurts and can do more damage.
fact: At A&E, the hospital's emergency department, a triage nurse checks everyone on arrival and the sickest go first, so a long wait means your injury is not the most dangerous one there.
fact: X-rays pass through skin and muscle but are stopped by bone, so bones show white and a crack shows as a dark line.
fact: One X-ray gives a tiny dose, about the same as a few days of natural background radiation; the radiographer who takes X-rays steps behind a screen because they take hundreds a day and the tiny doses would add up.
fact: If the broken ends have slid out of place, the doctor gives strong pain relief and pulls the bone back into line; this is called reducing a fracture.
fact: Breaks in many pieces or near a joint may need an operation with pins, plates or screws, done under anaesthetic (medicine that puts you to sleep so you feel nothing); the metal does not make you magnetic.
fact: Many breaks are silent, and a loud crack does not always mean a break.
fact: Shock and bad pain can make you faint for a moment; lying down with the legs raised helps the blood get back to the head.
fact: Growth plates near the ends of children's long bones make new bone; most growth plate breaks heal completely, and doctors often check them again later.
fact: Bone heals in stages, a blood clot, then soft new bone called a callus that slowly hardens, which takes several weeks even in children.
fact: A small child who suddenly will not walk on a leg should be checked by a doctor, even after a small fall.
fact: After a fall from a height the neck or back may be hurt, so the person is kept still and 999 is called.
exercise: Why do children's bones often break like a green twig? | They are bendier than adults' bones | They are softer than jelly | They are hollow like straws | They are made of a different material
exercise: What should you do first if you think you've broken your arm? | Hold it still and tell a grown-up straight away | Wiggle it to see if it still moves | Carry on playing and see if it gets better by itself | Rub it hard to warm it up
exercise: At A&E, why might a boy with a broken arm wait a long time? | The nurse sees the sickest people first | The nurses have forgotten about him | Broken arms are never treated on the same day | He arrived without a sticker
exercise: Why do bones show up white on an X-ray? | Hard bone stops the rays while soft parts let them through | Bones glow when light shines on them | The doctor colours them in afterwards | Bones are full of white paint
exercise: Is one X-ray of your arm dangerous? | No, the dose is tiny, like a few days of natural radiation | Yes, it can make your bones glow | Yes, so you can only have one a year | No, because X-rays are just ordinary light, like from a torch
exercise: Why does the doctor straighten a bone that is out of place? | Bones heal in the position they are held in | It makes the bone heal in only two or three days | It stops the bone from swelling for ever | It is the rule before every X-ray
exercise: Why might a broken leg need metal pins? | The break is in many pieces or near a joint | Metal makes all bones heal at least twice as fast | Every broken leg needs pins | The pins stop the leg growing too fast
exercise: Do you always hear a crack when a bone breaks? | No, many breaks are silent | Yes, every break makes a loud snap | Yes, but only grown-ups can hear it | No, bones only crack in cold weather
exercise: What helps a person who feels faint after a bad injury? | Lying down with the legs raised | Standing up and walking it off quickly | Drinking a fizzy drink as fast as you can | Sitting in a hot bath to warm up
exercise: What is a growth plate? | A soft area near the end of a bone where new bone is made | A metal plate doctors put in broken bones | The plate you must eat from to grow tall | A hard shield on top of the knee
exercise: Why does a broken arm take about six weeks to mend? | New bone has to form and slowly harden in stages | The cast takes six weeks to dry | Bones only heal on weekends | The doctor needs six whole weeks to find where the break is
exercise: Your little brother won't stand on his leg after a fall. What should happen? | A doctor should check it, even if the fall was small | Nothing, small children cannot break bones | He should be made to walk on it | Wait a month and see
exercise: A boy fell from the top of a high wall and is lying still. What should you not do? | Sit him up or pull him to his feet | Tell a teacher or another adult at once | Keep him still | Call for help
exercise: What is the only sure way to tell if a bone is broken? | An X-ray | Wiggling it | Listening for a crack | Seeing if it bruises
exercise: What does reducing a fracture mean? | Pulling the broken bone back into line | Making the broken bone shorter so it fits | Cutting the cast smaller so it fits better | Giving a smaller dose of medicine each day
exercise: Why does the radiographer step behind a screen? | They take hundreds of X-rays a day | One X-ray would hurt them badly | The machine might explode | They are not allowed to see your bones
test: my mate says if you can still move it, it ain't broken. true? | No, some broken bones can still move; only an X-ray tells | Yes, broken bones can never move | Yes, if it moves it is just bruised | No, broken bones move more than healthy ones
test: In a cartoon a dog breaks its leg and is running again the next morning. What is wrong? | Bones take weeks to heal, not one night | Dogs have no real bones inside their legs | Nothing, a leg really can heal overnight like that | Bones heal faster if you run around on them
test: Grandad says drink lots of milk and the bone mends in days. Right? | No, good food helps, but healing still takes weeks | Yes, milk glues bones in a day | Yes, three big glasses a day will heal any break in a week | No, milk makes bones break again
test: ugh why'd the nurse let that man go first, I was here before him! | He was sicker, and triage sends the most urgent first | He paid more money to go first | The nurse liked him better | He had a bigger sticker
test: What would happen if a broken arm were never straightened and held still? | It might heal crooked | It would grow a new arm | The bone would melt away | It would heal straighter than before
test: Sam hit the ground hard, felt sick and fainted for a second. Is he just a wimp? | No, shock and pain can make anyone faint | Yes, only weak people faint | Yes, fainting just means he is pretending to be hurt | No, fainting means the bone is fine
test: Which is true about growth plate breaks? | Most heal completely, but doctors may check them later | They always stop the bone growing | They never need an X-ray | They only happen to adults
test: Will the metal pins in my friend's leg make him stick to fridges? | No, pins do not make you magnetic | Yes, he will stick to magnets | Yes, but only when it rains | No, because the pins are made of wood
test: Your friend is lying at the foot of a tall wall and says his neck hurts. Best plan? | Keep him still and get an adult to call 999 | Help him stand up straight away | Carry him to the classroom | Give him a drink of water and sit him up against the wall
test: A greenstick fracture is called that because | The bone bends and cracks like a young green twig | The cast put on it afterwards is always coloured bright green | It happens when you fall on grass | It is a break in a green bone
test: I heard a massive crack when I came off my bike, so it's broken for sure? | Not for sure; loud cracks can be a sprain or the bike | Yes, a crack always means a break | Yes, and both bones are broken | No, real breaks are always silent
exam: Identify the investigation which most reliably establishes whether a bone has been fractured. | Radiographic imaging, commonly termed an X-ray | Manipulation of the affected limb by the patient | Auditory detection of a cracking sound | Observation of bruising over the site
exam: It is given that a child's fractured forearm has become displaced. State the principal reason for which the fracture is reduced. | Bone unites in whatever position it is held | Reduction accelerates healing to a matter of days | Reduction is required prior to any radiograph | Reduction prevents all subsequent swelling
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the order of treatment in an emergency department? | Patients are assessed on arrival and the most urgent treated first | Patients are treated strictly in the order in which they arrive at the door | Patients are treated in alphabetical order | Children are invariably treated before adults
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a person found after a fall from a considerable height. | The person ought to be kept still while emergency services are summoned | The person ought to be raised to a standing position | The person ought to be carried indoors without delay | The person ought to be given food and drink
